  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "About the Armenian alphabet",
      "body": "<p>Mesrop Mashtots created the Armenian alphabet around the year 405 so the Bible could be translated into Armenian. It had 36 letters; three more were added later. The script has been used without a break ever since, and the letters are a strong symbol of Armenian identity.</p>\n<p>Armenian is written left to right, with capital and small letters like Latin. The letters once also stood for numbers (Ա = 1, Բ = 2 …), which you still see in old books and clock faces.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Eastern and Western Armenian",
      "body": "<p>There are two standard forms of Armenian. Eastern Armenian is spoken in Armenia and Iran; Western Armenian by most of the diaspora in the Middle East, Europe and the Americas. They use the same letters but read some of them differently.</p>\n<p>The biggest difference is in three-way sets of consonants. Eastern says <span lang=\"hy\">բ, գ, դ</span> as b, g, d and <span lang=\"hy\">պ, կ, տ</span> as tight p, k, t. Western swaps them: <span lang=\"hy\">բ, գ, դ</span> are p, k, t and <span lang=\"hy\">պ, կ, տ</span> are b, g, d. So the name Պետրոս is Petros in the east and Bedros in the west.</p>\n<p>This app uses the Eastern values. Spelling also differs: Armenia uses a spelling reformed in the 1920s, while Western Armenian and Iranian Armenians keep the older classical spelling.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Three kinds of p, t, k",
      "body": "<p>Armenian has three versions of p, t, k, ts and ch:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>voiced</th><th>tight</th><th>with a puff</th></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"hy\">բ b</td><td lang=\"hy\">պ p</td><td lang=\"hy\">փ p'</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"hy\">դ d</td><td lang=\"hy\">տ t</td><td lang=\"hy\">թ t'</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"hy\">գ g</td><td lang=\"hy\">կ k</td><td lang=\"hy\">ք k'</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"hy\">ձ dz</td><td lang=\"hy\">ծ ts</td><td lang=\"hy\">ց ts'</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"hy\">ջ j</td><td lang=\"hy\">ճ ch</td><td lang=\"hy\">չ ch'</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The tight ones have no puff of air: hold a hand in front of your mouth and say spin, then pin. The tight sound is the one in spin. Many speakers make them ejective, with a little click from the throat.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "The unwritten ə",
      "body": "<p>Armenian often has a short ə sound between consonants that is not written. <span lang=\"hy\">խնձոր</span> (apple) is said khəndzor, and <span lang=\"hy\">գրել</span> (to write) is gərel.</p>\n<p>The letter <span lang=\"hy\">ը</span> is only written at the start of some words, and at the end of a word as the definite article: <span lang=\"hy\">տունը</span>, the house.</p>"
    }
  ]
